To avoid dealing with direct compatibility profile, NV and Chronos (and Magnificent) do roughly the following on top of each shader:

```
vec3 inPos = gl_Vertex.xyz;
vec2 inUV = gl_MultiTexCoord0.xy;
vec2 inLight = gl_MultiTexCoord2.xy;
```

Iris can't patch these, because they're global initializations before the `_vert_init();` function loads.

This is an alternative to DRAWBUFFERS needed to write to `colortex10` and above. Care must be taken to ensure that RENDERTARGETS and DRAWBUFFERS interact properly, ie that this type of file would be parsed the same as OptiFine:

```
void main() {
    /* DRAWBUFFERS: 013 */
    /* RENDERTARGETS: 0, 1, 2 */
}
```

In this example, the RENDERTARGETS directive comes later, and takes precedence, since DRAWBUFFERS and RENDERTARGETS are two different ways of setting the same values.

https://github.com/qouteall/ImmersivePortalsMod/blob/599ce5e1f6e531d5cfee8e57578f269c77c92b91/imm_ptl_core/src/main/resources/assets/immersive_portals/shaders/shader_transformation.json

Iris has some more robust shader patching code that allows it to handle less conventional main function declarations, even ridiculous things like this:

```glsl
void /* regexes don't work */ main()
